Looking at the diff: the regex alternation `years?` was changed to `years`, dropping the optional `s`. This means the singular unit "year" no longer matches the pattern at all.

In the original `ms@2.1.3`, `ms('1 year')` (and `ms('1year')`, `ms('-1 year')`, case-insensitively) matched via `years?` and returned `1 * 31557600000 = 31557600000`. After the change, "year" cannot be matched by any alternative (`yrs?` requires `yr`, `y` alone can't consume "ear" before the `$` anchor), so the regex fails, `parse` returns `undefined`, and the public function returns `undefined` for these inputs — a clear observable behavior change. The `case 'year':` in the switch is now unreachable dead code, confirming the intent of the original pattern was to support the singular form. The existing suite passing just means it only tests `'y'`/`'yrs'`/`'years'` plurals, not the singular.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('parses the singular unit "year"', function () {
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1 year'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1year'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1 YEAR'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('-1 year'), -31557600000);
  });
});
"}, "note": "The regex change from `years?` to `years` drops support for the singular unit 'year', which ms 2.1.3 previously parsed: ms('1 year') returned 31557600000 but now the regex fails entirely and the function returns undefined. The test asserts the original behaviour and fails on the patched build."}
```

